|~ `::error`, the kind every error is a value of, and what is declared
   under it: the refusal of a payload that is no map, the facts a refusal
   may carry, and the verbs of errors. The error at
   rest, the value a catch holds of a failure, is `::error{…}`, or the tag
   of the site that refused stacked over it, and its walk passes
   `::error` on its way to the map, so a verb declared here reaches every
   error.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:error
  |~~ An error is the facts of a failure as a value: what refused, on
      what, and the trace the failure left, under the tag of the site
      that refused, which names the page that tells how to repair the
      call. A step that fails answers an error raised, a failure; a
      reader learning a tool fails often, and the failure brings what
      the reader needs at the moment it is cheapest to give.

      An error at rest is written with a bang, `!{:reason :late}`, the
      tag of its site before the bang, `::Late!{:by 3}`; it is
      `::error{…}` under that tag, and prints with its bang, as a set
      prints with its hash. It is a value like any other: a container
      holds it, its facts read as fields, `!{:reason :late} | get /reason`
      answering `:late`, and JSON writes it as a value under its tag.

      Raised by `raise`, an error is a failure, which lives in the pipe
      alone; a host prints it as its error at rest, the trace it left
      among its facts, under the line
      `|~| raised, and no !| caught it`, which a reading passes over, so
      the print raised flies on from that trace; a query that does not
      parse flew no step and prints its facts alone.
      `|` passes a failure by, writing its trace, and `!|` alone catches
      it, its step running on the error at rest: `"x" | add 1 !| tag`
      answers the site, `::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError`, and
      `trace` the trace. No value holds a failure, so a literal, a name
      or a fact whose part fails answers the failure.
      ::snippet~(::error | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ The facts of the failure, a map. ~~| ::map
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ An error at rest holds the facts of a failure as a map,
            `!{:reason :late}`; a payload of another kind is refused, and a
            value of another kind is held as a fact, `!{:value [1]}`.
            ::link~(::error | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :name
        |~~ The name a reader asked for, a keyword or a tag, where nothing
            answers it.
        ~~|
        #[::keyword ::tag]
    | carries :addresses
        |~~ The addresses where a verb of that name lives, one for each
            kind that answers it.
        ~~|
        ::set
    | carries :nearest
        |~~ The names a step could have meant where it missed. For a name,
            a key or a tag nothing declares, those nearest in spelling:
            within a slip of the typing hand, an insertion, a deletion, a
            substitution or a swap of two neighbours, up to a third of its
            length and one at least, the nearest of them alone; beside
            them a name that begins it or that it begins, three letters at
            least, `multiply` for `mul`; and the verbs the page of a verb
            it begins with links whose names end as it goes on, `sumOf`
            for `addOf`. For a name whose verbs the kind of the subject
            lacks, the verbs their pages lead to that the subject answers
            and that answer a kind they answer, `print` and `json` for the
            `toString` of a vector. In the order of their spelling, and
            `[]` where none is near.
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | carries :repair
        |~~ The step the call was likely meant as, where a path stands
            instead of the value it names: the step again with the path
            read by `get`, `/tags | count` repaired as `get /tags | count`
            and `mul /qty` as `mul (get /qty)`.
        ~~|
        ::quote
    | carries :path
        |~~ The path to the part the refusal stands at: the path a read
            followed where it missed, the key or the index it missed named
            beside it, or the path to a number of a JSON text past the
            doubles.
        ~~|
        ::path
    | carries :index
        |~~ The index, from zero, of the element refused in a value no
            frame holds as its subject, an input, a payload, the args or
            the conditions of `allOf`, `anyOf` and `cond`; a part of the
            subject a step refused is named by the path of its frame.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | carries :expected
        |~~ The kinds the holder takes, where a value of none of them was
            refused, `#[::number]`.
        ~~|
        #[::tag]
    | carries :actualTag |~~ The kind of the value refused. ~~| ::tag
    | carries :arity
        |~~ The arity of the verb, how many args its inputs read, where
            the call gave more.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | carries :actualValue
        |~~ The value refused, but in a refusal of the subject its step was
            served, which holds its kind alone, the first frame of the trace
            holding the subject.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | carries :leftTag |~~ The kind of the value on the left. ~~| ::tag
    | carries :rightTag |~~ The kind of the value on the right. ~~| ::tag
    | carries :field
        |~~ The field of the record of a step that does not fit. ~~|
        ::keyword
    | proves :writesAnErrorAtRestByItsBang
        |~~ The bang writes an error at rest of the kind of errors. ~~|
        ~(!{:detail "boom"} | tag | eq ::error)
    | proves :standsOverItsKind
        |~~ Beneath the site's tag lies an `::error`. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| payload | tag | eq ::error)
    | proves :readsItsFacts
        |~~ An error's facts read as fields. ~~|
        ~(::error{:k 1} | get /k | eq 1)
    | proves :takesAFactOnTheWay
        |~~ A fact added on the fail track rides on. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| union {:request "r-1"} | raise !| get /request | eq "r-1")
    | proves :recoversByItsTag
        |~~ A failure of a known tag recovers. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| if (tag | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError) ~(0) ~(raise) | eq 0)
    | proves :raisesAgainOtherwise
        |~~ A failure of another tag raises again as itself. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| if (tag | eq ::Other) ~(0) ~(raise) !| tag | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector is no map of facts. ~~|
        ~(::error[1] !| tag | eq ::error/PayloadKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :standsWithATraceOfAnotherShapeAtRest
        |~~ An error at rest holds the facts it is written with, a trace of
            another shape among them, which a raise refuses.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~(!{:a 1 :trace 5} | get /trace) ~(!{:a 1 :trace 5} | raise !| tag)] | eq [5 ::trace/PayloadKindNotTakenError])
    | proves :repair/namesAWrittenPathAlone
        |~~ A path a name holds is no path the step wrote, and names no
            repair.
        ~~|
        ~(:p /qty | 5 | mul p !| query /repair | eq null)
    | proves :readsAnErrorAtRestAsItself
        |~~ The kind of errors over an error at rest is that error. ~~|
        ~(!{:by 3} | tag ::error | eq !{:by 3})

:error/trace
  |~~ Answers the trace of the error, the frames it left from the step
      that raised it to the `!|` that caught it, each the subject a step
      met and the steps from the one that failed:
      `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace` answers
      `::trace[::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)]]`, `add 1` failed on `"x"`
      and `mul 2` skipped. A step that ran the step that raised adds
      its frame after it, the `map` that ran an element among them, so
      the trace reads outward:
      `[1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | map ~failed` answers
      `[~(mul 2) ~(map ~(mul 2))]`. An error at rest that flew no step
      holds no `:trace` and answers `::trace[]`,
      `::error{:a 1} | trace`, and the `:trace` an error at rest is
      written with is brought to `::trace` as the verb answers it. The
      subject is an error at rest, the value a catch holds of a failure:
      under `|` a failure passes `trace` by, as it passes every step, and
      a value that is no error is refused, a map with the key `:trace`
      among them.
      ::link~(::trace | doc) ::link~(::frame | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The trace of the frames the error passed. ~~| ::trace
    | throws :SubjectNotErrorError
        |~~ `trace` reads the trace of an error at rest, the value a catch
            holds of a failure, `"x" | add 1 !| trace`; the key `:trace`
            of a plain map is read by `/trace`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersNothingForAnErrorThatFlewNoStep
        |~~ An error that flew no step holds no trace. ~~|
        ~(::error{:a 1} | trace | eq ::trace[])
    | proves :holdsTheStepThatRaised
        |~~ The trace of an error raised at once holds the one step. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | count | eq 1)
    | proves :meetsTheSubjectOfTheStep
        |~~ The frame holds the subject the step met. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | first | subject | eq "x")
    | proves :namesTheStepsSkipped
        |~~ The frame of the step that raised holds the steps skipped
            after it.
        ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | first | skipped | eq ~(mul 2))
    | proves :readsOutward
        |~~ The step that ran an element adds its frame after the
            element's.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | map ~failed | eq [~(mul 2) ~(map ~(mul 2))])
    | proves :bringsAWrittenTraceToItsKind
        |~~ The `:trace` an error at rest is written with is brought to
            `::trace`, a number refused as the answer.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~(::error{:trace [::frame[1 ~(pre)]]} | trace) ~(!{:trace 5} | trace !| tag)] | eq [::trace[::frame[1 ~(pre)]] ::error/trace/answer/KindNotTakenError])
    | proves :SubjectNotErrorError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map with the key `:trace` is no error. ~~|
        ~({:trace []} | trace !| get [~(tag) /actualTag] | eq [::error/trace/SubjectNotErrorError ::map])
    | impl ~(query /trace | coalesce ::trace[])

